  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction.\" The two reply texts are, in full, Reply A: \"```json\n{\"a\":1}\n```\n```json\n{\"b\":2} garbage\n```\" and Reply B: \"```json\n{\"a\":1}\n```\". For Reply A, the rule requires the answer {\"b\":2}, because the last object inside a code fence is {\"b\":2} in the second fence. For Reply B, the rule requires the answer {\"a\":1}, because that is the only object inside a code fence. The function recover_json_object returns {\"a\":1} for both replies: in Reply A the second fence's whole content is not a single valid JSON object, so the fenced pool skips it and returns the first fence's object, never considering the top-level object {\"b\":2} inside the second fence. This makes the two replies indistinguishable to the check. I would be wrong if recover_json_object(Reply A) returns {\"b\":2} (or any answer other than {\"a\":1}) when run on the actual harness.",
